Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Create a temporary array.
- Store all elements that are not equal to val.
- Copy the temporary array back to nums.
- Return the size of the temporary array.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize an empty temp vector.
2. Traverse nums.
3. If nums[i] != val, push it into temp.
4. Copy temp back to nums.
5. Return temp.size().

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeElement(vector<int>& nums, int val) {
        vector<int> temp;

        for (int num : nums) {
            if (num != val)
                temp.push_back(num);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < temp.size(); i++)
            nums[i] = temp[i];

        return temp.size();
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Use two pointers.
- One pointer traverses the array.
- Another pointer keeps track of the next valid position.
- Whenever an element is not equal to val, place it at the valid position.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize k = 0.
2. Traverse nums using i.
3. If nums[i] != val:
   - nums[k] = nums[i]
   - k++
4. Return k.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeElement(vector<int>& nums, int val) {
        int k = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != val)
                nums[k++] = nums[i];
        }

        return k;
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- Brute force is simple but requires extra space.
- The two-pointer overwrite approach is the most commonly expected solution because it preserves the relative order of remaining elements.
- The swap-with-last approach is optimal when order does not matter, as it performs fewer assignments when many elements equal val.
- Edge Cases:
  - Empty array.
  - All elements equal val.
  - No element equal val.
  - Single-element array.
- Follow-up:
  - Why does the optimal solution not increment left after swapping?
    - Because the swapped element hasn't been checked yet and may also equal val.
